parts.notes:
Read-only stock-take; nothing was built or changed.

1. Where the data is. The world file (crates/afm-engine/src/save.rs) holds every Person, Player and Alumnus field, hidden ones included. Some of those fields are pub(crate): ceilings, original potential, bg, family, visibility and gpa_trend. A reader outside the engine crate reaches them through the debug-feature accessors in debug.rs (ceilings, original_potential, person_background, alumnus_visibility). There is no accessor for Person.visibility or gpa_trend. Looks, fame, Dynamic Links, chemistry, drills and level-norm marks are not stored. The engine works them out when asked, so the reader has to link afm-engine to show them.

2. The export (crates/afm-tools/src/raw.rs; tend_by_year in bin/world_trial.rs) is a subset:
- people.jsonl covers college and prep players and high schoolers with 3+ stars. It leaves out birth dates, body, consistency, temperament, recovery, longevity, wear, Aura, followers, brand money, looks, background and family, spots, notes, and the season rows' team, depth and situation.
- hs_lines.jsonl covers only people past high school: 88,555 of 693,332 people with lines in trial 10.
- player_seasons.jsonl drops PlayerSeason.snaps.
- tend_by_year covers only the future years, not the history years.

3. People are deleted when they leave. World::compact deletes everyone who leaves football unless he was drafted, became a pro, or had 4+ stars. Most past high school and college careers are therefore gone, which blocks full-funnel and full-class checks for history years.

4. History is missing for most stored values. Per player, the world keeps only today's numbers for:
- the 67 attributes and their ceilings (each season row keeps only start and end overall and potential);
- body weight;
- tendencies, traits, mood, trust, followers and brand money;
- injuries, which have no type in the yearly roll; college and high school engine injuries are not kept;
- life events (only 150 days are kept), court cases (only the newest 120) and ties (dropped ties are deleted);
- star ratings (only the latest).

5. The 'why' behind each change is not stored. The yearly step's factors (age window, coaching boost, snaps, work ethic, morale, form, potential drive terms, event sizes) and each position move's fit numbers are worked out and then thrown away. These are the main needs_new items if the reader is to explain every change.

6. Real references in the repo, for my part:
- data/real-world/combine_by_position.csv, draft_positions_2000_2025.csv, development_injuries.json, tendency_spreads.json, rates.json, trial3_baselines.json (name repeats, draft picks by star), talent_by_state.csv, college_game_targets.json (season leaders), engine_targets.json and position_value.json;
- data/names/;
- docs/research/development-and-injuries.md and media-and-social.md;
- the test thresholds in crates/afm-tools/tests/national_class.rs;
- the constants cited in calendar/born.rs (CDC, NCES) and world/looks.rs (Bosch 2014, Deurenberg 1998).

No real data exists in the repo for: high school stats, family and background, mood, life-event rates, college trouble, follower counts by level, face and appearance rates, position-change rates, or race shares by position. A few real numbers in the checks are from memory and are marked 'to check' or put under needs_new.
